  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Why a USB Drive Never Hits Its Label",
  "hero.sub": "An NVMe SSD rated at 7,250 MB/s, put into a USB enclosure,<br>why does it measure only a few hundred MB/s to two or three GB/s?",
  "hero.lead": "The second question follows: what do “USB 3.2 Gen 2×2” and “40Gbps” printed on the box actually mean? Below, we follow one copy from the computer to the flash: who decides on the USB wire, how a name maps to a line rate, how a read command is packed into 31 bytes, why doing one thing at a time is slow, what the chip in the enclosure translates, why a drive slows down partway through a copy, which route Thunderbolt and USB4 take instead, and finally flash drives and memory cards. <strong>Each model is either a rule from a specification, a table from one, or a textbook result</strong>, scaled down to run in a web page.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The numbers from the specifications (frame formats, tables, rates) are taken from the original texts of USB 2.0, USB 3.2, USB4 and the mass storage class specifications. <strong>Where a value is marked “tuned parameter”, it was chosen so the model can be computed, not measured</strong>, and the text says how far the conclusion moves when that value is halved or doubled. This page covers mechanisms and specifications only, and <strong>does not rate any enclosure or drive as a purchase</strong>.",

  "toc.ch1": "Where it sits on the map",
